id: rule-assign-wrong
title: Wrong precondition
topic: Assignment
example-of: ASSIGN
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule ASSIGN to decide whether this triple can be derived.

given:
  Assignment expression: $y + 1$

pre: y = 2
post: y = 2

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  y := y + 1     // i1
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: The substitution
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  The precondition is obtained by substituting the variable $y$ with the
  assignment expression $y + 1$ in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := y + 1, Q)
  = (y = 2)[y <- y + 1]     // Q is y = 2
set: R1 = (y = 2)[y <- y + 1]

step: Apply the substitution
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  Every $y$ in $y = 2$ becomes $y + 1$.
calc:
  (y = 2)[y <- y + 1]
  = y + 1 = 2               // y replaced by y + 1
set: R1 = y + 1 = 2

step: Simplify
rule: LOGIC
focus: R1
text:
  We subtract 1 from both sides of the equation.
calc:
  y + 1 = 2
  = y = 1                   // subtract 1 from both sides
set: R1 = y = 1

step: P does not imply R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  The precondition $P$ is a different formula. The consequence rule would
  need $P$ to imply $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: y = 2 ==> y = 1
status: fails
counterexample: y = 2
why:
  From the state $y = 2$ the assignment gives $y = 3$, so $Q$ is false at
  the end. The triple is not valid.
